  "body": {
    "id": "thm:maximal-common-group",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The group \\(G_\\star\\) is the direct product of the symmetric groups on the \\(\\sim\\)-classes. Consequently, for \\(K=2\\), \\(G_\\star\\cong(S_2)^{\\binom{m+1}{2}}\\); for \\(K\\geq3\\), \\(G_\\star\\cong S_2\\), acting only on \\(\\{C_+,C_-\\}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Gstar",
      "equiv",
      "K",
      "m",
      "Cplus",
      "Cminus"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:maximal-common-group",
      "def:probability-equivalence",
      "thm:alphabet-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "This translates the probability partition into the largest schedule-universal transformation group without presupposing the group.",
    "gap": "\\cite{HemerikGoeman2018} establishes exact testing for a specified invariant group; the new input is the maximal group forced by the composite causal null.",
    "consumer": "It supplies a precise validation target for any software option advertised as a weak-null common-invariance randomization test.",
    "proof_tex": "For a bijection \\(f\\) of \\(\\mathcal X_{m,K}\\), Definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}maximal\\text{-}common\\text{-}group}\\) says\n\\[\n f\\in G_\\star\n \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n P_R(f(C))=P_R(C)\\ \\text{for every }C\\in\\mathcal X_{m,K}\n \\text{ and }R\\in\\mathcal R_0(n,K).\n\\]\nBy Definition \\(\\mathrm{def{:}probability\\text{-}equivalence}\\), the right-hand side is exactly the assertion that \\(f(C)\\) belongs to the \\(\\sim\\)-class of \\(C\\) for every \\(C\\).  Since the profile space is finite and \\(f\\) is bijective, this is equivalent to saying that \\(f\\) restricts to an arbitrary permutation of each equivalence class.  Conversely, every collection of within-class permutations preserves every \\(P_R\\) by the definition of the classes.  Composition and inversion act coordinatewise, so\n\\[\n G_\\star\\cong\\prod_{A\\in\\mathcal X_{m,K}/\\sim} S_A.     \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(K=2\\), a histogram of arm size \\(m\\) is determined by its count of outcome \\(1\\), so \\(|\\mathcal X_{m,2}|=(m+1)^2\\).  Exactly \\(m+1\\) profiles satisfy \\(C=C^\\top\\).  Theorem \\(\\mathrm{thm{:}alphabet\\text{-}frontier}\\) says that every other class is a two-point transpose class, whose number is\n\\[\n \\frac{(m+1)^2-(m+1)}2=\\frac{m(m+1)}2=\\binom{m+1}{2}.   \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe singleton factors in (1) are trivial, and (2) yields\n\\(G_\\star\\cong(S_2)^{\\binom{m+1}{2}}\\).  When \\(K\\geq3\\), the same theorem gives one two-point class \\(\\{C_+,C_-\\}\\) and only singleton classes otherwise.  Equation (1) then gives \\(G_\\star\\cong S_2\\), with its sole nontrivial action exchanging the two corners.",
